---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Measuring a GUI app without lying to yourself — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="You cannot measure the speed of a desktop app by clicking through it, because you never do the same thing twice. This post shows the small tool that I built instead, and the three reports that changed what I worked on."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-15">September 15, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Measuring a GUI app without lying to yourself</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          You cannot measure the speed of a desktop app by clicking through it,
          because you never do the same thing twice. This post shows the small
          tool that I built instead, and the three reports that changed what I
          worked on.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 4 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I made the thumbnail grid faster, but it was already fast. The real
          wait came when the app opened a RAW file. A mode that runs the same
          fixed script and prints timings showed me this in its first report.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="runs-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-run="hand">Run by hand</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-run="script">Run --profile</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 186" role="img" aria-label="Dot plot of six runs of the same benchmark. Six runs by hand have a wide spread on the time axis. Six scripted --profile runs have almost the same value.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">SIX RUNS OF THE SAME &ldquo;BENCHMARK&rdquo;</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="52">Clicks by hand</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="70">in the app</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="176" y1="56" x2="624" y2="56"></line>
          <g data-row="hand">
            <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="433" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="268" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="598" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="192" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="521" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="341" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          </g>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" data-spread="hand" d="M192 76 V84 M192 80 H598 M598 76 V84"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="395" y="102" text-anchor="middle">the spread comes from you</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="136">--profile</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="154">the same script, always</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="176" y1="140" x2="624" y2="140"></line>
          <g data-row="script">
            <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="397" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="401" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="392" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="404" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="399" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="395" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          </g>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="398" y="172" text-anchor="middle">a change that you can see</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="624" y="172" text-anchor="end">time &rarr;</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">Six runs each. Press a button to run again from zero.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Hand runs never repeat, so I could find any meaning in my before
          and after numbers. The script repeats.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>A benchmark has to give the same answer twice.</strong> So I
          script the steps, even if the script is less realistic than a real
          session.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write down why the test does what it does.</strong> "120
          thumbnails, because that is two screenfuls" is a reason that someone
          can question. "120" alone is only a number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Measure memory as well as time.</strong> No timing report
          showed the function that caused 47% of the memory use.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why I could not just use the app</h2>
      <p>
        The usual method is to attach a profiler (a tool that records where
        the program spends its time) and click through the app for thirty
        seconds. That finds things that are badly broken. It does not tell me
        if a change made the app better, because I cannot do the same thing
        twice. On one run, I reached row 40 with an empty thumbnail cache. On
        the next, I reached row 31 with a full cache, because the first run
        filled it.
      </p>

      <h2>Run the code without the window</h2>
      <p>
        My fix was a command-line flag. <code>--profile &lt;folder&gt;</code>
        runs the real code on that folder: it lists the folder and loads
        photos, the catalog, and thumbnails. Then it prints timings and exits.
        It never opens a window, so it does not use the UI library or the
        graphics card.
      </p>
      <p>
        The script fills thumbnails for 120 photos, which is approximately two
        screenfuls. Then it opens ten photos in sequence, the way you step
        through a shoot with the arrow keys. Each number has a comment in the
        code that explains it. The full
        mode is approximately 230 lines. It is not clever. It only gives me
        the same number twice.
      </p>

      <h2>Keep it out of the version that you download</h2>
      <p>
        Small markers attach the timing code to functions. Only two build
        settings switch it on, and both are off by default. With them off, the
        markers leave each function exactly as it was, so the release build
        contains none of the timing code and the browser build does not get
        any bigger. One surprise: the profiling library must be a normal
        dependency, not an optional one, because the markers have to compile
        in every build, even when they do nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>The first report</h2>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 196" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: listing the folder takes 3 to 5 milliseconds. Filling 120 thumbnails from a warm cache takes 22 milliseconds. A RAW preview takes 11 milliseconds. A sharp RAW decode takes 170 to 210 milliseconds.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">300 PHOTOS, WARM CACHE</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="51">List the folder</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="34" width="9" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="245" y="51">3 to 5 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="91">Fill 120 thumbnails</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="74" width="46" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="282" y="91">22 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="131">Open a RAW, preview</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="114" width="23" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="259" y="131">11 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="171">Open a RAW, sharp</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="226" y="154" width="400" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="238" y="171">170 to 210 ms &middot; all of the wait is here</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          I made the grid faster. The report showed me in approximately four
          seconds that the wait was somewhere else.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The report also showed a fix that I would never find by reading the
        code. The app prefetches the quick, low-quality preview of the next
        photo, but not the slow, sharp decode. So when you step through a
        folder of RAW files with the arrow keys, you wait the full 200 ms every
        time, even though the app knew half a second earlier which photo came
        next.
      </p>

      <h2>The second report</h2>
      <p>
        The profiler can also count the memory that each function allocates,
        instead of the time it takes. One function allocated 12.0 MB out of
        25.3 MB, which is 47% of all the memory the app allocated in that
        session. It was the function that hands thumbnails to the UI library
        to draw.
      </p>
      <p>
        The UI library can only build its image type by copying the pixels
        into fresh memory, even though the pixels were already in the loader's
        cache in exactly the right layout. So I wrote them directly to the
        graphics card instead. That took the function from 12.0 MB to 0.16 MB,
        and the whole session from 25.3 MB to 13.4 MB.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing was slow. But the browser build runs as WebAssembly, and
        WebAssembly memory never shrinks once it has grown. So memory that the
        app needs for only a moment stays allocated for as long as the tab is
        open.
      </p>

      <h2>The third report</h2>
      <p>
        On 200 photos with a warm cache, decoding all 200 thumbnails across 8
        workers took 39.5 ms in total. Analysing one photo took 7.39 ms, so
        1.48 seconds for the batch. The analysis runs on the main thread and
        cannot be parallelized across the processor's cores, so it costs about
        fifty-five times as much as the decoding.
      </p>
      <p>
        My plan had been to make decoding faster and to prefetch it. Neither
        change would have made a noticeable difference.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/never-load-the-whole-folder.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Never load the whole folder</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/my-plan.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">My plan, and what is not in it</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>

  <script>
    const fig = document.getElementById("runs-demo");
    if (fig) {
      const svgNS = "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
      const readout = fig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      // Dot positions on the time axis: the same six values as the static plot.
      const rows = {
        hand: { cy: 56, cls: "lp-fig-bad", xs: [433, 268, 598, 192, 521, 341] },
        script: { cy: 140, cls: "lp-fig-bar", xs: [397, 401, 392, 404, 399, 395] },
      };
      const handNotes = [
        "You scrolled fast and reached row 40. The thumbnail cache was empty.",
        "You reached row 31. The cache was full, because run 1 filled it.",
        "A different scroll and a different cache state.",
        "A different scroll and a different cache state.",
        "A different scroll and a different cache state.",
        "A different scroll and a different cache state.",
      ];
      const count = { hand: 6, script: 6 };

      function spread(key) {
        const xs = rows[key].xs.slice(0, count[key]);
        const path = fig.querySelector(`[data-spread="${key}"]`);
        if (!path) return;
        if (xs.length < 2) { path.setAttribute("d", ""); return; }
        const lo = Math.min(...xs), hi = Math.max(...xs);
        path.setAttribute("d", `M${lo} 76 V84 M${lo} 80 H${hi} M${hi} 76 V84`);
      }

      function run(key) {
        const g = fig.querySelector(`[data-row="${key}"]`);
        if (count[key] >= 6) { g.replaceChildren(); count[key] = 0; }
        const row = rows[key];
        const dot = document.createElementNS(svgNS, "circle");
        dot.setAttribute("class", row.cls);
        dot.setAttribute("cx", row.xs[count[key]]);
        dot.setAttribute("cy", row.cy);
        dot.setAttribute("r", "6");
        g.appendChild(dot);
        count[key] += 1;
        if (key === "hand") spread("hand");
        const n = count[key];
        readout.textContent = key === "hand"
          ? `Run ${n} by hand: ${handNotes[n - 1]}`
          : `Run ${n} of --profile: 120 thumbnails, then 10 photos. The same steps as every run.`;
      }

      fig.querySelectorAll("[data-run]").forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener("click", () => run(btn.dataset.run));
      });
    }
  </script>
</Base>
